<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tomas Masaryk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk (1850–1937) was a Czech philosopher and politician who led the campaign abroad for Czechoslovak independence during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. He became the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1918 and was re-elected three times before retiring in 1935, shaping the country's democratic and liberal political culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk.jpg" alt="Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tom%C3%A1%C5%A1_Garrigue_Masaryk_1925.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
